> The repo is ready for Pages. I can't create the project myself — no Cloudflare
credentials here — so these are your dashboard steps.

1. Create the Pages project

Cloudflare dashboard → Workers & Pages → Create → Pages → Connect to Git → 
select pejdo/web:

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Build command | npm run build |
| Output directory | dist |
| Production branch | main |

2. Set up Resend (before the form can send)

- resend.com → add domain pejdo.com → add the SPF/DKIM records it gives you in 
Cloudflare DNS. Without these, mail is rejected or lands in spam.
- Create an API key.

3. Add four variables to the Pages project (Settings → Environment variables → 
Production):

| Name | Value | Type |
|---|---|---|
| RESEND_API_KEY | your re_… key | Secret |
| CONTACT_TO | nikola@pejdo.com | Plaintext |
| CONTACT_FROM | web@pejdo.com | Plaintext |
| PUBLIC_CONTACT_FORM_ENDPOINT | /api/contact | Plaintext |

That last one is the switch that moves the form off mailto:. Leave it unset 
until the Pages deploy is confirmed working, so the form never 404s again.

4. Move the domain — once the Pages deploy serves correctly on its *.pages.dev 
URL, add pejdo.com as a custom domain there and remove it from the old Worker. 
Test on pages.dev first; that way nothing user-facing breaks if something's off.

I found and fixed one thing that would have broken your very first Pages build: 
Astro 7 requires Node ≥22.12, and Pages defaults to an older version when a 
project doesn't pin one. There was no pin, so the build would have failed 
immediately. Added .node-version and an engines field.

I also verified the function properly this time, executing the real handler with
a stubbed mail API rather than just reading the source — 24 checks, all 
passing. Confirmed: valid submissions send with the visitor in Reply-To and your
domain in From; CRLF is stripped from the subject so a crafted input can't 
inject a Bcc; <script> in the message is escaped in the HTML part; invalid input
sends no mail at all; the honeypot silently discards; provider errors return a 
generic 502 without leaking detail; and oversized messages are capped at 5,000 
characters.

Wrangler wouldn't stay running in this environment, so the one thing still 
unverified is the function under Cloudflare's actual runtime. Once the Pages 
deploy is up, curl -X POST https://your-project.pages.dev/api/contact should 
return a 303 redirect rather than a 404 — tell me what you get and we'll confirm
